=== Does the role-A proof amortise across swaps? -- LaZer ===

Proof system : LaZer (LNP22 linear relation with norms), post-quantum
Relation     : exists r : A r = t and ||r||inf <= 1   (per instance)
Statement    : block-diagonal, 6 x 23 polynomials per instance
Repetitions  : 5 per batch size, after an untimed warm-up
k=1 uses the COMMITTED parameter set configuration 3 ships.

  measured k = 1  (proof 30723 B)
  measured k = 2  (proof 46751 B)
  measured k = 4  (proof 76191 B)
  measured k = 8  (proof 141157 B)

--- Cost of ONE proof covering k statements (totals) ---

    k            prove (ms)           verify (ms)     proof (B)
    1         158.8 +-  73.3          75.2 +-   4.2         30723
    2         276.2 +-  22.3         218.6 +-   8.4         46751
    4         931.8 +-  92.2         692.7 +-  27.6         76191
    8        3560.5 +- 354.5        2676.4 +- 255.1        141157

--- Amortised PER SWAP (total / k) ---

    k      prove (ms)     verify (ms)       proof (B)      proof vs k=1
    1           158.8            75.2         30723.0             1.00x
    2           138.1           109.3         23375.5             0.76x
    4           233.0           173.2         19047.8             0.62x
    8           445.1           334.6         17644.6             0.57x

--- What this shows ---

Unlike Groth16, whose proof is a CONSTANT 128 B, LaZer's proof grows with k
-- but SUBLINEARLY, so per-swap size does fall: 0.57x at k=8.
That is a saving on a cost that actually mattered: the proof is the dominant
communication object of configuration 3, where Groth16's 128 B never was.

BUT IT IS A TRADE, NOT A WIN, AND THE TRADE IS BAD HERE. Per-swap compute
goes the other way and goes further: prove+verify per swap is 3.33x at k=8
(prove 159 -> 445 ms, verify 75 -> 335 ms). LaZer's work grows
SUPERLINEARLY in the batch, so each extra instance costs more than the last.

For configuration 3 that settles it. evidence/stage2/latest shows the role-A
proof is already 98.6% of end-to-end time, so the binding constraint is
COMPUTE, not communication -- and batching buys communication by spending
compute. Trading 43% of the proof bytes for 3.3x the per-swap proof time
makes the dominant cost worse to improve a subordinate one.

Taken with the Groth16 run, BOTH provers answer the amortisation question
negatively, for opposite reasons: Groth16's 1/k is perfect but lands on a
cost that was already negligible; LaZer's lands on a cost that matters but
is paid for in the one that matters more.

Against that, batching costs the same protocol concessions it costs for any
proof system: the batch must be proved BEFORE any of its statements is used,
so a party has to know its next k swaps in advance; every counterparty
verifies the whole batch to use one statement of it; and the statements in a
batch become linked, since anyone seeing two of them knows they were proved
together. None of those is priced above.

SCOPE: time and communication only. The batched parameter sets were generated
by the same LaZer codegen as the committed k=1 set and target the same
knowledge error, but have NOT been independently reviewed, and no security
claim about batching is made here. Configuration 3 still uses k=1.
